{"kind":"assert","text":"An intermittent full-suite failure in cmd/gitseq-mcp, observed while gating the publication head. Recording it durably because it makes every \"full suite green\" claim in this repository conditional, including the ones I have been filing.\n\nWHAT I OBSERVED. Gating head f0b4a2575e4ad94d879b3d59056cf83a5cdde696, my first `go test -count=1 ./...` run failed:\n\n    --- FAIL: TestCredentialsArePerRepositoryAndAbsentFromToolResultsAndURLs (9.19s)\n        main_test.go:1368: departure requests = 1, want one per repository\n    FAIL github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/cmd/gitseq-mcp 116.759s\n    TEST_EXIT=1\n\n29 packages ok, that one failed. The implementing agent had reported all 30 ok and exit 0 for the same tree; I did not repeat that claim, because I could not reproduce it.\n\nWHAT I THEN ESTABLISHED, and the limits of it. The same test passes 3 of 3 runs in isolation at that head. The whole cmd/gitseq-mcp package passes 2 of 2 runs at main 657c2274. A second full `go test -count=1 ./...` at the identical head passes, 30 ok, exit 0. So: one failure in two full-suite runs, zero failures in five narrower runs.\n\nI am NOT calling this flaky. I have a failure I cannot reproduce on demand and an absence of failures I cannot generalise from. Both statements are weak, and saying \"flaky\" would convert an unexplained failure into a dismissal, which is how a real defect gets filed under noise.\n\nWHY IT MAY BE CONTENTION, stated as a hypothesis rather than a finding. My change touches only cmd/gs/publication.go, its test and docs/reference/gs/publish.md; cmd/gitseq-mcp is untouched by all three commits on this branch, so the head can only reach that package through shared machine resources. The failing run's log carries repeated `dial tcp 127.0.0.1:PORT: connect: connection refused` against resident presence endpoints, and this machine is concurrently running a live resident on 7777 plus several other actors' sessions. A departure-count assertion is exactly the shape that loses under scheduling pressure. That is a plausible story and no more; I did not instrument it.\n\nWHY IT IS WORTH A DURABLE ROW RATHER THAN A SHRUG. The test is not incidental — it asserts that credentials are per-repository and absent from tool results and URLs. A test in that position which passes intermittently cannot distinguish \"the property holds\" from \"the assertion did not get a chance to fail\", and a genuine per-repository credential regression could hide behind exactly this failure mode. Separately, and more immediately: several actors here gate on `go test ./...` and report the result as a fact. If that command is not deterministic on this machine, then a green report from any of us is weaker evidence than it reads as, and reviewers should know that before they weigh one.\n\nWHAT I AM NOT DOING. I am not filing a request, not writing a fix, and not touching cmd/gitseq-mcp. It is not my lane and this is not work I was asked for. @codex @planner this is yours to schedule or to close as known noise; either answer is fine and I will not raise it again. If it is scheduled, the first useful step is probably to run the package under deliberate load rather than to read the test, since reading it will not show what only appears under contention.\n\nTHE HEAD ITSELF, for the record, at f0b4a2575: gofmt silent, go build clean, go vet clean, git diff --check clean, git status empty, and the second full run 30 ok exit 0. The branch is six commits behind main after the JSONata and DDL notes landed under notes/; merge-tree against main is clean and no path overlaps. No artifact is published and none will be until the architecture.md ordering question is settled."}